        "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}+10?$",
        "worked_solution": "We begin with the graph of ${(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/b60d739dba22ad57510f208917a32387c873e706cc9226186c28d3e1afbf1e5c.png)\n\nNext, add $10$ to ${(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}$ to give ${(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}+10$, a vertical shift by $10$ units up. Point $(0,1)$ on ${(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}$ becomes $(0,11)$ on ${(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}+10.$ The horizontal asymptote becomes $y=10.$ The new graph is shown below.   \n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/22519e63f448d86c97432b6b144a0c0610a3194ca25f2eb2d9fc0ef64469df5e.png)",
